  "habitat": "Almost any open water with fish: rivers, perennial streams, dams, lakes, estuaries, coastal lagoons and rock pools. Hovers often, so it depends less on overhanging perches than other kingfishers. It leaves waters that dry up.",
  "summary": "A boldly black-and-white, crested kingfisher with a stout black bill. It often hovers over open water before plunge-diving. It is the only kingfisher in the region where the sexes differ in their breast-bands: two bands in the male, one (often broken) in the female.",
  "sampleData": false,
  "similar": [
    {
      "slug": "megaceryle-maxima",
      "note": "Giant is much larger (43–46 cm vs 28–29 cm), with fine white spotting on black upperparts and chestnut on the breast (male) or belly (female). Pied is pure black and white and hovers habitually (Newman's; eBird)."
    }
  ],
